c it actually accomplishes this  so well that it makes me wonder why most  animated features are so overly  concerned with any kind of sense of  realism rather than trying to craft  their own individual style but then  again that's what I have always loved  about trigger and they're talented staff  they are one of the very few companies  out there willing to take absurd visual  creative risks and have the proper  backbone to pull it off and make the  rest of the anime world look tame by  comparison now that isn't to say that  there are no curves or circles at all in  premiere there definitely are but they  are very strategically used as how I  would put it I guess for the most part  they act as accents to various objects  well they are made to deliberately stand  out like one scene in the film where our  main characters are eating pizza the  pizza itself obviously being circular  but it becomes a true focal point of the  scene due to that simple factor and  there's a lot more but the general idea  is that shapes are thought about  on my new Overland premiere and I think  this very simple bit conscious decision  allows for a flood of pure creativity  and that might be because one of my  personal mantras has always been that  restrictions breed creativity and  premiere provides the ultimate  restriction being the use of straight  lines and sharp angles 95% of the time  and so it results in design solutions  that very few individuals would ever  land on due to never having the  inclination to engage in a core  reviewing of the world around them that  really is the only true revolutionary  aspect of premiere though if you were to  strip all of that away you would still  have a solid film but if you're familiar  with the work of those currently at  trigger then you won't necessarily find  anything new to take from premiere and I  don't necessarily mean that as a bad  thing either however it is exceptionally  evident in a purposeful the premiere  exists as something of a 